---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import TreeFig from '../../components/TreeFig.astro';
import TermsLab from '../../components/TermsLab.astro';
import GenTree from '../../components/GenTree.astro';
import PinScript from '../../components/PinScript.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';
import CodeWalk from '../../components/CodeWalk.astro';
import Peek from '../../components/Peek.astro';
import TreeBuilder from '../../components/TreeBuilder.astro';
import { train, phoneFiles, folderTree, pathTree, listNodePic, treeNodePic } from '../../lib/illus.js';
import { treeNodeCode, treeNodeSteps, buildCode, buildSteps } from '../../lib/walks.js';
import { node, svgTree } from '../../lib/tree.js';
import { course } from '../../data/itc120.js';
import { hi } from '../../lib/code.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const listNode = String.raw`class Node {
  int data;
  Node next;     // one link
}`;
const SPEC = 'A(B(D,E(H,I(J)),F),C(G))';
const depthPic = pathTree({ spec: SPEC, focus: 'E', paths: [{ ids: ['A', 'B', 'E'], color: '#0e6b63', wash: '#d7ece9', nums: true }], label: 'Depth of E: count the edges from the root A down to E: 2' });
const heightPic = pathTree({ spec: SPEC, focus: 'E', paths: [{ ids: ['E', 'I', 'J'], color: '#e8662e', nums: true }], faded: [['E', 'H']], notes: [{ x: 58, y: 252, text: 'shorter:', size: 13, color: '#37474a' }, { x: 58, y: 268, text: 'not counted', size: 13, color: '#37474a' }], label: 'Height of E: count the edges on the longest path down from E: E to I to J, 2 edges. E to H is shorter and does not count.' });
const explorerPeek = pathTree({ spec: SPEC, focus: 'B', paths: [['B', 'D'], ['B', 'E'], ['B', 'F']].map((ids) => ({ ids, color: '#e8662e' })), label: 'The explorer tree, with B and its three children highlighted' }) + '<p class="small">B has three children: D, E, and F.</p>';
const listPeek = `<pre class="code"><code>${hi(listNode)}</code></pre>` + listNodePic();
const leftTree = node(50); leftTree.l = node(30);
const rightTree = node(50); rightTree.r = node(30);
const slotPic = (t, label) => svgTree(t, { showNil: true, tags: { 50: 'root' }, label, minW: 160 });
const build = String.raw`TreeNode root = new TreeNode(50);
root.left = new TreeNode(30);
root.right = new TreeNode(70);
root.left.right = new TreeNode(40);`;

const quiz = [
  { q: 'Which node has no parent?', opts: ['A leaf', 'The root', 'Any internal node', 'Every node has a parent'], a: 1, why: 'The <b>root</b> is the top node and the only node with no parent.' },
  { q: 'A node with no children is called:', opts: ['A root', 'A leaf', 'An internal node', 'A sibling'], a: 1, why: 'A <b>leaf</b> has no children. An internal node has at least one.' },
  { q: 'In the explorer tree, what is the depth of E?', opts: ['1', '2', '3', '0'], a: 1, why: 'Depth counts edges from the root down: A → B → E is <b>2</b> edges.' },
  { q: 'In the explorer tree, what is the height of E?', opts: ['0', '1', '2', '3'], a: 2, why: 'Height takes the <b>longest</b> path down: E → I → J is <b>2</b> edges. E → H is only 1, so it does not count.' },
  { q: 'A tree has 12 nodes. How many edges does it have?', opts: ['12', '11', '13', '24'], a: 1, why: 'Every node except the root has one edge up to its parent: 12 − 1 = <b>11</b>.' },
  { q: 'Which statement about a binary tree is true?', opts: ['Every node has exactly 2 children', 'Every node has at most 2 children', 'It must be sorted', 'It cannot have leaves'], a: 1, why: 'A binary tree allows <b>0, 1, or 2</b> children per node. Sorting is the extra rule of a <i>binary search</i> tree (next lesson).' },
  { q: 'In Java, what does <code>root.left.right</code> refer to?', opts: ['The right child of the root', 'The left child of the root\'s right child', 'The right child of the root\'s left child', 'The root itself'], a: 2, why: 'Read the dots from left to right: start at root, go <b>left</b>, then go <b>right</b>.' },
  { q: 'Are <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> like <code>previous</code> and <code>next</code> in a linked list?', opts: ['Yes, left goes back and right goes forward', 'No, both point down to children', 'Yes, but only in binary search trees', 'No, they point to the parent'], a: 1, why: 'Both <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> point <b>down</b>, to the node\'s two children.' },
];
---
<Base title="Trees and Binary Trees · ITC 120" description="Trees in plain English: root, parent, child, leaf, depth, height, and binary trees with left and right children." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Midterm › Trees and BSTs</p>
    <p class="kicker">Trees and BSTs · Lesson 1 of 6</p>
    <h1>Trees and Binary Trees</h1>
    <p class="lede">Our first non-linear data structure: nodes that branch out, like folders inside folders.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">In this lesson, you will:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>connect linked-list nodes to tree nodes;</li>
        <li>use tree terminology: root, parent, child, sibling, leaf, ancestor, descendant, subtree, depth, height;</li>
        <li>recognize a <b>binary tree</b> and read its <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> references in Java.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/linked-lists/'}>Linked lists lesson</a>, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="lists">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · From lists to trees</p>
      <h2>Some data branches out</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>You already know nodes from linked lists. Think of a <b>train</b>: each car is a node. It carries cargo (its <b>data</b>) and has <b>one</b> hook (<code>next</code>) to the car behind it. The last car has nothing hooked to it, so its <code>next</code> is <code>null</code>.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus" set:html={train()} />
      <p>In Java, that car is this class: one value, one link.</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(listNode)} /></pre>
      <p>But some data does not line up in one chain. Open the Files app on your phone: <i>Internal storage</i> holds <i>DCIM</i>, <i>Download</i>, and <i>Documents</i>, and <i>DCIM</i> holds <i>Camera</i> and <i>Screenshots</i>. One folder can hold <b>many</b> folders. Draw it out, and it branches like an upside-down tree:</p>
      <div class="fig illus-fig">
        <div class="illus-row">
          <figure class="illus" set:html={phoneFiles()} />
          <p class="illus-eq" aria-hidden="true">same folders, drawn as a tree</p>
          <figure class="illus" set:html={folderTree()} />
        </div>
        <p class="illus-cap">The folder at the top is the <b>root</b>. A folder inside another is its <b>child</b>. Folders with nothing inside are <b>leaves</b>. You will learn all these terms in Part 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Tree</p>
        <p>A <b>tree</b> is a non-linear data structure made of nodes connected by edges, arranged in levels from top to bottom. It follows three rules:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>One node is at the top: the <b>root</b>.</li>
          <li>Every other node has exactly <b>one parent</b>.</li>
          <li>A node can have any number of <b>children</b>.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Where you see trees in IT</p>
        <p>Folders on a computer, the categories in an online shop (Electronics → Phones → Android), an organization chart, and a family tree are all trees.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="words">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Terminology</p>
      <h2>Tree terminology</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Trees come with their own vocabulary. Most words come from family trees. Explore them first, then read the summary table below.</p>
      <TermsLab id="terms" spec={SPEC} />
      <p>Here are all the terms, with examples from the explorer tree. <b>Tap any row</b> to see it in the tree (on a computer, just point at the row). The colors in the Example column match the colors in the tree.</p>
      <div class="terms-ref" id="terms-ref">
        <aside class="tr-side" aria-label="The explorer tree, for reference">
          <GenTree spec={SPEC} label="The explorer tree: A at the top; B and C; D, E, F under B; G under C; H and I under E; J under I" />
          <p class="tr-note small">Orange = the term. Gold = the related nodes.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="tr-main scroll-x">
          <table class="tbl tr-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Node</b></td><td>holds one value</td><td><span class="w-hl">A</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="" data-hl2="" data-e="A-B"><td><b>Edge</b></td><td>the <b>line</b> that links a parent to its child (only the line, not the nodes)</td><td>the <span class="w-hl">line</span> between A and B</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H,I,J" data-hl2="" data-e="A-B,A-C,B-D,B-E,B-F,C-G,E-H,E-I,I-J"><td><b>Size</b></td><td>the number of nodes</td><td><span class="w-hl">10</span> nodes (and 9 edges)</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Root</b></td><td>the top node; the only one with no parent</td><td><span class="w-hl">A</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="B" data-hl2="D,E,F" data-e="B-D,B-E,B-F"><td><b>Parent / child</b></td><td>a node directly above / below another</td><td><span class="w-hl">B</span> is the parent of <span class="w-hl2">D, E, F</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="D,E,F" data-hl2="B,C,H,I" data-e=""><td><b>Siblings</b></td><td>nodes with the same parent</td><td><span class="w-hl">D, E, F</span> (children of B); also <span class="w-hl2">B and C</span>, and <span class="w-hl2">H and I</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="D,H,J,F,G" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Leaf</b></td><td>a node with no children</td><td><span class="w-hl">D, H, J, F, G</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,B,C,E,I" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Internal node</b></td><td>a node with at least one child</td><td><span class="w-hl">A, B, C, E, I</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,B,E" data-hl2="H" data-e="A-B,B-E,E-H"><td><b>Ancestor / descendant</b></td><td>going down from X to Y: X is an ancestor of Y, Y is a descendant of X</td><td><span class="w-hl">A, B, and E</span> are ancestors of <span class="w-hl2">H</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="B" data-hl2="D,E,H,I,J,F" data-e="B-D,B-E,B-F,E-H,E-I,I-J"><td><b>Subtree</b></td><td>a node plus everything below it</td><td>the subtree rooted at <span class="w-hl">B</span>: B, <span class="w-hl2">D, E, H, I, J, F</span></td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="E" data-hl2="A,B" data-e="A-B,B-E"><td><b>Depth</b> of a node</td><td>edges from the root <b>down to</b> the node</td><td>depth of <span class="w-hl">E</span> = 2 (<span class="w-hl2">A</span> → <span class="w-hl2">B</span> → E)</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="E" data-hl2="I,J" data-e="E-I,I-J"><td><b>Height</b> of a node</td><td>edges on the <b>longest</b> path from the node <b>down to a leaf</b></td><td>height of <span class="w-hl">E</span> = 2 (E → <span class="w-hl2">I</span> → <span class="w-hl2">J</span>; E → H is shorter, so it does not count)</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A" data-hl2="B,E,I,J" data-e="A-B,B-E,E-I,I-J"><td><b>Height of the tree</b></td><td>the height of the root</td><td>4 (<span class="w-hl">A</span> → <span class="w-hl2">B → E → I → J</span>)</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="J" data-hl2="A,B,E,I" data-e="A-B,B-E,E-I,I-J"><td><b>Depth of the tree</b></td><td>the depth of its deepest node: the same number as the height of the tree</td><td>4 (<span class="w-hl">J</span> is the deepest node)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Do not mix them up: depth vs. height</p>
        <p><b>Depth</b> looks <b>up</b> to the root: how far down is this node? <b>Height</b> looks <b>down</b> to the leaves: how tall is the tree under this node? Both count <b>edges</b>, not nodes. The root has depth 0; every leaf has height 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Depth of E = 2</p><div set:html={depthPic} /><p class="small">Count the edges from the <b>root</b> down to E: A → B → E. There is only one way up, so depth is easy.</p></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Height of E = 2</p><div set:html={heightPic} /><p class="small">Count the edges from E down to its <b>deepest</b> leaf: E → I → J. E → H is only 1 edge. Height always takes the <b>longest</b> path down.</p></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="binary">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Binary trees</p>
      <h2>At most two children</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Binary tree</p>
        <p>A <b>binary tree</b> is a tree in which every node has <b>at most two</b> children: a <b>left child</b> and a <b>right child</b>.</p>
      </div>
      <p>So <Peek label="the explorer tree from Part 2" title="The explorer tree from Part 2" html={explorerPeek} /> is <b>not</b> a binary tree: <b>B has three children</b> (D, E, and F). One node with three or more children is enough to break the rule. In a binary tree, every node has 0, 1, or 2 children.</p>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Left and right are two different slots</h3>
        <p>Every node in a binary tree has two <b>separate</b> child slots: <code>left</code> and <code>right</code>. Think of your left and right shoes: they are a pair, but you cannot swap them. A child always sits in one <b>specific</b> slot, and a slot with nothing in it is <code>null</code> (the small dashed boxes below).</p>
        <div class="two-col nil-light">
          <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Tree 1</p><div set:html={slotPic(leftTree, '30 is in the left slot of 50; the right slot is empty')} /><p class="small" style="text-align:center">30 is in the <b>left</b> slot.<br /><code>root.left</code> is 30, <code>root.right</code> is null.</p></figure>
          <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Tree 2</p><div set:html={slotPic(rightTree, '30 is in the right slot of 50; the left slot is empty')} /><p class="small" style="text-align:center">30 is in the <b>right</b> slot.<br /><code>root.left</code> is null, <code>root.right</code> is 30.</p></figure>
        </div>
        <p>Both trees hold the same two values, 50 and 30. But a program that asks for <code>root.left</code> gets 30 from Tree 1 and <code>null</code> from Tree 2. Same values, different slots: these are two <b>different</b> binary trees.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>A binary-tree node in Java</h3>
        <p>Each node is an object with one value and two child references. Compare it with <Peek label="the linked-list Node class from Part 1" title="From Part 1: a linked-list node" html={listPeek} />: <code>next</code> became <code>left</code> and <code>right</code>. Step through the class to see what each line means:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="walk-class" code={treeNodeCode} steps={treeNodeSteps()} cap="Reading the TreeNode class" />
        <p>Here is the difference in pictures, then in a table:</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Linked-list node</p><Fragment set:html={listNodePic()} /></figure>
          <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Binary-tree node</p><Fragment set:html={treeNodePic()} /></figure>
        </div>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Singly linked list</th><th>Binary tree</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>head</code> refers to the first node</td><td><code>root</code> refers to the top node</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>next</code>: one link</td><td><code>left</code> and <code>right</code>: two links</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>null</code>: no next node</td><td><code>null</code>: no child on that side</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="trap">
          <p class="note-label">Careful</p>
          <p><code>left</code> and <code>right</code> are <b>not</b> previous and next. Both point <b>down</b>, to children. A missing child is <code>null</code>; a leaf has both sides <code>null</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Building a tree in Java</h3>
        <p>To build a tree, make nodes with <code>new</code> and store them in the child slots. Step through it: watch which slot each line fills.</p>
        <CodeWalk id="walk-build" code={buildCode} steps={buildSteps()} cap="Building a tree, line by line" />
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Now you: read the code, then draw the tree</p>
        <p class="small muted">No walkthrough this time. Draw it on paper first, then check.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(build)} /></pre>
        <p>Tip: in the code, read each dot (<code>.</code>) from left to right. <code>root.left.right</code> means: start at <code>root</code>, go to its left, then go to that node's right.</p>
        <p>Draw it on paper first. Then build your drawing below and tap <b>Check my tree</b>.</p>
        <TreeBuilder id="build-check" values={[50, 30, 70, 40]} target="50(30(,40),70)" cap="Build your drawing" />
        <details class="reveal">
          <summary>Check your drawing</summary>
          <div class="flow">
            <TreeFig spec="50(30(,40),70)" label="50 at the root, 30 on its left, 70 on its right, 40 is the right child of 30" />
            <p>40 is the <b>right</b> child of 30. 30 has no left child (<code>null</code>).</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Same values, different shapes</h3>
        <div class="two-col">
          <figure class="fig"><TreeFig values={[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70]} label="A bushy tree of height 2" /><p class="small" style="text-align:center">height 2</p></figure>
          <figure class="fig"><TreeFig values={[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70]} label="A chain of height 6" /><p class="small" style="text-align:center">height 6: shaped like a chain</p></figure>
        </div>
        <p>The same seven values can make a short, bushy tree or a tall chain. Shape matters: you will see why in the next lessons (and again in AVL trees).</p>
        <TreeBuilder id="build-shapes" values={[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70]} cap="Build-a-tree challenges" hint="Drag values onto the dashed spots (or tap a value, then tap a spot). Tap a leaf to take it back. The orange line shows the longest path from the root: that is the height." challenges={[
          { say: 'Use all 7 values and make the tree as <b>short</b> as possible (height 2).', size: 7, height: 2 },
          { say: 'Use all 7 values and make the tree as <b>tall</b> as possible (height 6).', size: 7, height: 6 },
          { say: 'Use 5 values and make a tree of height 3.', size: 5, height: 3 },
        ]} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="practice">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Label this tree</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="paper flow">
        <TreeFig spec="M(K(B,L),T(R(Q,S),))" label="Practice tree: M at the root; K and T below it; B and L under K; R under T; Q and S under R" />
        <ol>
          <li>Root?</li><li>Leaves?</li><li>Parent of R?</li><li>Sibling of B?</li><li>Ancestors of S?</li>
          <li>Depth of L?</li><li>Height of T?</li><li>Size? Edges?</li><li>Is this a binary tree? Why?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal">
          <summary>Show the answers</summary>
          <div>
            <ol>
              <li>M</li><li>B, L, Q, S</li><li>T</li><li>L</li><li>R, T, M</li><li>2</li>
              <li>2 (T → R → Q)</li><li>8 nodes, 7 edges</li><li>Yes: every node has at most 2 children (T has only one).</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-trees" questions={quiz} />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A binary-tree node holds data, a left reference, and a right reference.</li>
          <li>The root is the top node. A leaf has no children.</li>
          <li>Depth: edges from the root to the node. Height: edges from the node down to its deepest leaf.</li>
          <li>A binary tree: every node has at most 2 children.</li>
          <li>A missing child is null.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> <a href={base + '/itc120/bst/'}>Binary search trees and BST search</a>.</p>
      <div class="refs small muted">
        <p class="note-label">References</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Programiz, "Tree Data Structure" and "Binary Tree." <a href="https://www.programiz.com/dsa/trees">programiz.com/dsa/trees</a></li>
          <li>N. Dale, D. T. Joyce, and C. Weems, <i>Object-Oriented Data Structures Using Java</i>, 4th ed. Jones &amp; Bartlett Learning, 2018.</li>
          <li>mycodeschool, "Data structures: Introduction to trees" and "Binary tree" [Videos], YouTube.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<PinScript />

<style>
  .refs .note-label { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--step-0); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
  .refs ol { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; }
</style>
